From: Andreas Wendleder <andreas.wendleder@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 23:12:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH RFC 02/13] wifi: b43: reprogram the MAC filter after an
 interface restart

b43_op_start() clears wl->filter_flags, but mac80211 computes @changed
for b43_op_configure_filter() against the flags it cached from the
previous run. After a down/up it can therefore report no change, so
b43_adjust_opmode() is not called and MACCTL keeps the cleared filter.

Seen on a BCM4360: after an interface restart nothing was received until
some other change touched the filter. Compare the requested flags with
what the hardware was actually programmed with and add the difference to
@changed.

Not checked on other chips.
---
 drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c
index 8f4aff3..57a5935 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c
@@ -4318,6 +4318,12 @@ static void b43_op_configure_filter(struct ieee80211_hw *hw,
 		   FIF_OTHER_BSS |
 		   FIF_BCN_PRBRESP_PROMISC;
 
+	/* b43_op_start() zeroes wl->filter_flags, but mac80211 computes
+	 * @changed against its own cached flags, so after an interface
+	 * restart it reports no change and MACCTL kept the zeroed filter.
+	 * Compare against what the hardware was actually programmed with. */
+	if (wl->filter_flags != *fflags)
+		changed |= wl->filter_flags ^ *fflags;
 	wl->filter_flags = *fflags;
 
 	if (changed && b43_status(dev) >= B43_STAT_INITIALIZED)
-- 
2.54.0

